;; llvmi's eval level's classes (bin/llvmi check; doc/llvm/LLVM-CROP-SURVEY.md, "As built: llvmi (LL2's run, LL6, LL7)"), also AOT's
;; after test/llvm/divergences-aot.edn's: every row of test/llvm/check-{eval,aot}-linux-amd64.edn that does not pass, in goi's format
;; (GR10): a row is in the first class that names it in :tests or one of whose :match regexps finds its "RESULT REASON". :deferred, the
;; lowering's or ci's breadth still to build (LL5's list, survey §5.4, or C's crop); :design, by the survey's decisions. Read with the
;; Clojure reader.
[
 {:id :personality
  :title "modules that unwind: C++'s exceptions"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "a module with a personality, invoke, landingpad or resume needs its personality's runtime (C++'s: libsupc++ lowered with the pending-flag layer, bin/cppi's runtime-build), which llvmi does not link on ci yet; the lowering takes these modules (bin/cppi lower-check runs them with gcc)"
  :decided "survey §5.3 (the personality as a parameter), LLS15 (a)"
  :match [#"^refused personality: "]}
 {:id :intrinsic
  :title "intrinsics outside the lowering's table"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the -O2 and C23 intrinsics the lowering's table lacks (llvm.fshl, llvm.scmp, llvm.lround, ...): the table grows by what the run corpora reach"
  :decided "survey §5.4, §6, LLS10 (a)"
  :match [#"^refused intrinsic: "]}
 {:id :datalayout
  :title "another target's data layout"
  :kind :design
  :why "llvmi runs x86-64 SysV's IR; a module written for another data layout (32-bit pointers) is read and printed, not run"
  :decided "survey §6 (class target), LOWER.md §7"
  :match [#"^refused datalayout: "]}
 {:id :target-type
  :title "target extension types"
  :kind :design
  :why "target(\"spirv.Event\") and its kind have no C type; another target's types are read and printed, not run"
  :decided "survey §6 (class target)"
  :match [#"^refused type: .*\(target "]}
 {:id :ci-builtins
  :title "ci's builtins: fmodf and the __atomic_* family"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the lowering writes frem as __builtin_fmodf and atomics as gcc's __atomic_* builtins (LOWER.md §7), which gcc takes and ci's builder does not have yet (arbace/c/build.ae's builtin-aliases lacks fmodf and fmodl; no __atomic_fetch_*): the program faults at the call"
  :decided "C's crop (doc/C23-SURVEY.md §6); this survey's Proposed"
  :match [#"undefined function (__builtin_fmod|__atomic_)"]}
 {:id :abi-punning
  :title "a lowered function called by native code through another type"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the lowering types i32 as unsigned (LOWER.md §7); musl's qsort calls the lowered comparator as int (*)(const void *, const void *): gcc's code reads the same register, ci converts the unsigned result to the pointer's int as a value, so -1 compares as 4294967295 and the sort differs"
  :decided "this survey's Proposed"
  :tests ["c-O0.synth.ext-callbacks" "c-O2.synth.ext-callbacks"]}
 {:id :ub-refinement
  :title "undefined behaviour refined otherwise"
  :kind :design
  :why "the program converts a NaN to an unsigned integer (C23 6.3.1.4p1: undefined; LangRef: fptoui of NaN is poison); the lowering refines poison as gcc's conversion, lli's JIT as its own (evidence: the program's own name and the conversions it prints)"
  :decided "survey §6, LLS9 (a)"
  :tests ["c-O0.run.nan-to-unsigned" "c-O2.run.nan-to-unsigned"]}
 {:id :argv0
  :title "argv[0]"
  :kind :design
  :why "the program prints its arguments: lli's argv[0] is the module's path, ci's the program's name"
  :decided "survey §5.2"
  :tests ["ee.MCJIT.2002-12-16-ArgTest"]}
 {:id :crash-report
  :title "both runs crash: the crash reports differ"
  :kind :design
  :why "the test expects arguments its RUN line passes (lli's run here passes none): both runs fault with the same status, and stderr holds each one's own crash report (lli's stack dump, cirun's or the AOT build's fault line)"
  :decided "survey §5.2"
  :match [#"^stderr .*(cirun|ciaot): fault: .* \| lli: PLEASE submit a bug report"]}
 {:id :long-double
  :title "x86_fp80 in memory: ci's long double is binary64"
  :kind :design
  :why "the lowering writes x86_fp80 as C's long double, which ci holds as a double (8 bytes, LDBL_MANT_DIG 53: doc/c/SPEC.md §12.5, CS35): an aggregate holding one has another size than the data layout's 16 bytes (the lowering's static assertions fail), and the 80-bit arithmetic a program checks differs"
  :decided "C's crop, CS35 (ci's long double); survey §6"
  :tests ["tx-O0.20040709-1" "tx-O2.20040709-1" "tx-O0.ieee.copysign2" "tx-O2.ieee.copysign2" "tx-O0.memcpy-2" "tx-O2.memcpy-2"
          "tx-O0.strlen-1" "tx-O2.strlen-1" "tx-O0.20080502-1" "tx-O2.20080502-1"]}
 {:id :lli-reference
  :title "the reference fails: lli's JIT"
  :kind :design
  :why "lli's own run is not the module's meaning: its JIT resolves every external at once (a function declared and defined nowhere, link_error or foo, which gcc's tests expect the optimizer to remove, is a link error before main; llvmi runs on, never calling it or faulting at the call), it crashes (a stack dump where clang 23 -O0's build of the module runs: the reference's :clang second opinion), or the module is written for lli's interpreter (-force-interpreter: a va_list one pointer wide, `alloca ptr`, which x86-64's ABI, the JIT's and llvmi's, overruns with its 24-byte va_list: llvmi prints `result is 4`, not the CHECK line's `result is 6`; LL6's note that llvmi met it was wrong, the own verdict of LL9a found it). Since LL9a the modules with an own verdict are judged by it, not by lli: what is left here is the interpreter's module"
  :decided "survey §5.2, LLS8 (a) (the IR's own build as the reference; lli as the brief took it)"
  :tests ["ee.Interpreter.test-interp-variable-arguments"]
  :match [#"\| lli: JIT session error: Symbols not found" #"^status status 0, lli's 139: .*lli: PLEASE submit a bug report" #"^stderr stderr 0 bytes, lli's [0-9]+: +\| lli: PLEASE submit a bug report"]}
 {:id :dg-options
  :title "the torture test's dg-options not in the pack's command"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "gcc's harness compiles a torture test with its dg-options and dg-additional-options (20040409-3w.c: -fwrapv, the signed overflow it tests wraps; restrict-1.c: -fgnu89-inline, so its inline foo has an external definition); the pack's IR (test/llvm/ir/tx.cmd) was made with one command for every file, so the IR is another program's: the 3w variant's overflow is undefined and folded to abort(), restrict-1 calls a foo no unit defines. Judged by the test's own verdict (exit 0) since LL9a; lli's run failed the same way before (the :lli-reference class held them)"
  :decided "doc/llvm/LLVM-CROP-SURVEY.md, \"As built: LL9a\" (bin/llvmi ir-make to pass each test's dg-options when the pack is next made, by hand with clang)"
  :tests ["tx-O0.restrict-1" "tx-O2.restrict-1" "tx-O0.20040409-3w" "tx-O2.20040409-3w"]}
 {:id :envp
  :title "main's third argument: ci's runs have no environment"
  :kind :design
  :why "the module reads envp[0] (main(argc, argv, envp)); lit runs it with lit's environment, ci starts every program with an empty one (go/arbace/ci/prog.ae's RunMain: argv, an empty environment and the auxiliary vector), so that a run does not depend on the host's variables, and strlen(envp[0]) faults"
  :decided "doc/llvm/LLVM-CROP-SURVEY.md, \"As built: LL9a\"; C's crop's runtime (go/arbace/ci)"
  :tests ["ee.MCJIT.2003-08-21-EnvironmentTest"]}
 {:id :stackrestore
  :title "llvm.stackrestore: dynamic allocas not released"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "llvm.stacksave and llvm.stackrestore lower to nothing, so a loop's variable-length arrays (__builtin_alloca_with_align on ci's C stack) are released only at the function's return: a long loop overflows ci's stack where the IR's own build reuses it"
  :decided "survey §5.4 (the lowering's breadth)"
  :match [#"stack overflow \(alloca\)"]}
]
